allenge","question_type":"application","question":"Evaluate $\\int_0^1 2x\\cos(x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ using the Type 1 substitution.","options":["$\\cos 1$","$2\\sin 1$","$1-\\cos 1$","$\\sin 1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"31509f91b7d7925ee08bca5f"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Using $t = x^3+2x+3$, which transformed integral equals $\\int_1^2 \\frac{3x^2+2}{x^3+2x+3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$?","options":["$\\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_6^{15} \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_6^{15} t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$","$\\int_1^2 \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4557b5c5de01a7a5d05a1739"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What new variable form starts Type 1 substitution?","options":["$x=\\varphi(t)$","$t=g(x)$","$y=f(x)$ only","$n\\to\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9672092cd4e2a517b4790f47"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which component should already appear for Type 1 substitution to be effective?","options":["A missing endpoint","A second independent variable only","A derivative component $g'(x)$","A negative exponent only"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8745b3b7d2790353d9115727"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_integration_by_substitution_type_1_60c41bec_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is strict monotonicity useful in the Type 1 theorem?","options":["It makes $g'(x)=0$ everywhere","It removes the need to transform limits","It makes the integrand constant","It typically ensures invertibility of $g$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"356e1451cf1dad81031ac503"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_ca